spec:
  grid: 19x19
  colors_input: black(0), grey(5), red(2) only
  background: random mix of grey and black; grey 49-62%, black 35-47%
  marker_windows:
    count: 4
    structure: each is a 3x3 region where all 9 cells are non-black, center is grey, and 1-6 cells are red (rest grey)
    placement: non-overlapping (no shared cells between any two selected windows)
    red_pattern: arbitrary placement within the 8 non-center cells (center always grey)
    separation: min Chebyshev distance between windows >= 4 [observed]
  red_pixels: all red pixels in grid belong to exactly one selected marker window's 3x3 area; total 10-16
  non-selected_candidates: 0-3 additional candidate windows whose red set is a strict subset of a selected window's red set (caused by adjacent overlapping views)
  decoy_windows: 0-3 all-foreground 3x3 windows with grey center but no red [observed]
  output_transform: |
    For each selected window: center grey -> yellow(4), other grey cells -> orange(7), red unchanged.

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first (build marker windows, embed in random background)
  steps:
    1. Create 19x19 grid filled randomly with grey(5) at ~55% and black(0) at ~45%.
    2. Choose 4 non-overlapping 3x3 positions (min Chebyshev distance >= 4).
    3. For each window, pick red count k in 1-6, place k red pixels in the 8 non-center cells (rest grey); center stays grey.
    4. Force all 9 cells of each window to be non-black (set any black cell to grey or red per pattern).
    5. Ensure no accidental all-foreground 3x3 windows elsewhere have red pixels that would create a candidate whose red set is NOT a strict subset of a selected window. Easiest: ensure no red pixels exist outside the 4 windows (already guaranteed by step 3).
    6. Optionally allow 0-3 decoy all-grey 3x3 windows by placing solid grey patches (or accept natural ones from random fill).
    7. Verify the maximal-marker-window algorithm yields exactly the 4 intended windows.
